major.recommendation:
Mirror the resendVerification pattern: add a Redis SET-NX-EX rate-limit key (e.g. forgot:rate:{email} TTL 60s and forgot:rate:ip:{ip} threshold ~10/15min) executed unconditionally before the user lookup so timing remains uniform. Pseudocode:

  return redisTemplate.opsForValue()
      .setIfAbsent(forgotKey(email), "1", FORGOT_RATE_TTL)
      .onErrorResume(err -> { log.warn(...); return Mono.just(true); })
      .flatMap(ok -> { if (Boolean.FALSE.equals(ok)) return Mono.empty(); /* still 200 — never reveal rate-limit state */ return doForgotPassword(...); });

Note: returning 429 here would itself be an enumeration oracle (timing differs); silently dropping the request and still returning 200 preserves anti-enumeration. Combine with a per-IP counter that DOES surface 429 for clearly abusive volumes (e.g. >30/15min from a single IP).

Make the two branches do equivalent work. Two options:

Option A (preferred — cheap, mirrors login pattern): on the unknown/deleted branch, also perform a no-op Mongo round-trip of comparable cost (e.g. userRepository.findByEmail(email).then() is already done; additionally do a save of an unrelated document or schedule a Mono.delay calibrated to median save latency). The cleanest implementation is to enqueue a fixed Schedulers.boundedElastic delay matching observed p50 of the known-user save+email-build, applied uniformly. Example:

  Mono<Void> uniformDelay = Mono.delay(Duration.ofMillis(50)).then();
  // append .then(uniformDelay) on every branch.

Option B (stronger): always do the userRepository.save() with the same fields wrapped in a transactional rollback, OR always perform a SecureRandom + SHA-256 (these are cheap but constant cost). This narrows the gap but does not fully equalize Mongo round-trip timing.

In either case, add an integration assertion (or a unit timing assertion using a controllable clock) so regressions don't sneak back in.
